.\" zshrsall.1 — comprehensive reference for zshrs (like zshall(1)).
.\" Combines the per-topic content that, in upstream zsh, is split
.\" across zshbuiltins, zshoptions, zshparam, zshzle, zshcompsys etc.
.\" The zshrs-specific layers (daemon, fusevm, AOT, AOP, caches,
.\" parity modes, recorder) are documented inline because they have
.\" no upstream counterpart.
.TH ZSHRSALL 1 "2026-05-14" "zshrs 0.11.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zshrsall \- the Z shell, Rust compiled implementation (full reference)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zshrs
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ argument
\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
This is the comprehensive reference for
.BR zshrs (1).
For the short option summary, see
.BR zshrs (1).
For the singleton coordinator daemon, see
.BR zd (1).
.PP
.B zshrs
is a strict 1:1 port of the Z shell
.RB ( zsh (1))
into Rust, with an additional non-port extensions layer that adds
features zsh does not have. The runtime is a four-crate workspace:
.IP "" 4
.B zshrs
\(em the runtime binary
.IP "" 4
.B compsys
\(em completion-system subcrate
.IP "" 4
.B zshrs-daemon
\(em the singleton coordinator
.IP "" 4
.B zshrs-recorder
\(em the optional shell-session recorder
.PP
.B src/ported/
holds the strict 1:1 ports (89 files, every function maps to a real
.IB Src/ x .c
zsh function, enforced by
.BR tests/port_purity.rs ).
.B src/extensions/
holds zshrs-only code (31 files: AOT, daemon coordination, plugin and
script and autoload caches, fish-style autosuggestions / abbreviations /
syntax highlighting, the persistent worker pool, ZWC bytecode helpers).
.SH "THE COMPILED PIPELINE"
Every command zshrs runs traverses this pipeline:
.IP "" 4
.B lex
\(em
.IR src/ported/lex.rs ,
matches
.IR Src/lex.c .
.IP "" 4
.B parse
\(em
.IR src/ported/parse.rs ,
matches
.IR Src/parse.c .
.IP "" 4
.B emit
\(em wordcode emission, then
.B fusevm
opcode lowering.
.IP "" 4
.B compile
\(em
.B fusevm
JIT compiles hot units to native machine code on the second invocation.
.IP "" 4
.B run
\(em the bytecode runs in
.B fusevm
with a persistent worker pool servicing builtins and most external
calls without
.BR fork (2).
.PP
The pipeline outputs can be inspected directly via
.BR \-\-dump\-tokens ,
.BR \-\-dump\-ast ,
.BR \-\-dump\-wordcode ,
and
.BR \-\-disasm
(see
.BR zshrs (1)).
The resulting bytecode is cached as rkyv shards under
.IR ~/.cache/zshrs/ .
.SH "INVOCATION"
.B zshrs
accepts the same argument grammar as
.BR zsh (1).
A login shell sources login files, an interactive shell sources
.IR .zshrc ,
.BR \-c
takes a command literal,
.B \-s
forces stdin reading, and bare-script invocation
.RB ( "zshrs " script )
runs the script with
.B $0
set to its path. See
.BR zshrs (1)
for the full flag table.
.SH "PARITY MODES"
.TP
.B \-\-zsh
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/zsh .
All caches disabled, daemon disabled. Used by the parity test corpus
.RB ( tests/zsh_compat_parity_gaps.rs )
to verify byte-for-byte equivalence.
.TP
.B \-\-bash
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/bash .
.TP
.B \-\-ksh
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/ksh
(ksh-93 dialect).
.TP
.B \-\-posix
Identical behaviour to
.BR /bin/sh
(Bourne, dash).
.PP
Parity modes never write to the cache and never connect to the daemon;
they exist for compatibility testing and for environments where
caching must be off.
.SH "STARTUP FILES"
Same set, order, and gating as
.BR zsh (1):
.PP
.IR /etc/zshenv " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zshenv " \(em " /etc/zprofile " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zprofile
(login)
.B \(em
.IR /etc/zshrc " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zshrc
(interactive)
.B \(em
.IR /etc/zlogin " \(em " $ZDOTDIR/.zlogin
(login)
.B \(em
.IR $ZDOTDIR/.zlogout " \(em " /etc/zlogout
(login shutdown).
.PP
When
.B $ZDOTDIR
is unset,
.B $HOME
is used. The
.B RCS
and
.B PRIVILEGED
options gate sourcing identically to upstream zsh.
.SH "FILES, LANGUAGE & SYNTAX"
zshrs accepts the full zsh language: parameter expansion (including
.BR ${(z)var} ", " ${(s/X/)var} ", " ${(q)var} ", " ${(P)var} ", " (V) ", " (kv) ", " (ZX) " ..."),
process substitution
.RB ( <(...) ", " >(...) "),"
brace expansion, glob qualifiers
.RB ( "*(.D)" ", " "*(om[1,5])" ", " ...),
extended-glob patterns
.RB ( **/* ", " "(foo|bar)" ", " "<NUM-NUM>" "),"
arithmetic
.RB ( "(( ... ))" ", " $((...)) "),"
math functions, and conditional expressions
.RB ( "[[ ... ]]" "),"
all matching upstream zsh. Compatibility surface for plugins
.RB ( "oh-my-zsh, " "prezto, " "zinit, " "p10k, " "zsh-autosuggestions"
etc.) is sacred; the
.B 96-test
invariants
.RB ( tree_walker_absent ,
.B no_tree_walker_dispatch )
plus the parity corpus pin observable behaviour.
.SH "ZLE \(em ZSH LINE EDITOR"
All 193 widgets from upstream
.B Src/Zle/iwidgets.list
are ported: history navigation, vi find/repeat/marks, undo/redo,
isearch, yank-pop, shell-aware word motion, region/visual mode, text
objects, completion menu, and
.B $zle_highlight
parsing. The lexer-driven shell-word splitter is the same code path
.BR zsh-syntax-highlighting
expects; tokenisation matches
.BR Src/glob.c::zshtokenize
exactly.
.SH "COMPLETION"
The completion system (compsys) ships as its own crate
.RI ( compsys/ )
with the per-shell-state caches mmap'd from
.IR ~/.cache/zshrs/ .
.BR _approximate ,
.BR _expand_alias ,
.BR _list ,
.BR _menu ,
.BR _oldlist ,
and the
.B *.zwc
autoload-bundle path are honoured. The
.B zsh-more-completions
corpus (16,806 files) loads unchanged.
.SH "BUILTINS"
Every builtin from upstream zsh is implemented: the full
.BR alias " " bg " " bindkey " " break " " builtin " " bye " " cap " " cd " " chdir " " clone " " command " " compadd " " compcall " " compctl " " compdescribe " " compfiles " " compgroups " " compquote " " compset " " comptags " " comptry " " compvalues " " continue " " declare " " dirs " " disable " " disown " " echo " " echotc " " echoti " " emulate " " enable " " eval " " exec " " exit " " export " " false " " fc " " fg " " float " " function " " functions " " getln " " getopts " " hash " " history " " integer " " jobs " " kill " " let " " limit " " local " " log " " logout " " noglob " " popd " " print " " printf " " private " " pushd " " pushln " " pwd " " r " " read " " readonly " " rehash " " return " " sched " " set " " setopt " " shift " " source " " stat " " suspend " " test " " times " " trap " " true " " ttyctl " " type " " typeset " " ulimit " " umask " " unalias " " unfunction " " unhash " " unlimit " " unset " " unsetopt " " vared " " wait " " whence " " where " " which " " zcompile " " zformat " " zftp " " zle " " zmodload " " zparseopts " " zpty " " zregexparse " " zsocket " " zstat " " zstyle " " zsystem
set, plus 48 fish-ported builtins surfaced under the same dispatch.
See
.BR zshbuiltins (1)
for option-by-option detail; all options listed there are honoured.
.SH "OPTIONS"
zshrs honours every option defined in upstream zsh:
.BR setopt / unsetopt
manipulate the same on/off table, the same default emulation matrix
.RB ( ksh ,
.BR sh ,
.BR zsh )
applies, and the
.BR emulate (1)
builtin switches between them at runtime. See
.BR zshoptions (1)
for the full list.
.SH "DAEMON COORDINATION"
When run in full mode (no parity flag), zshrs cooperates with a
singleton coordinator process,
.BR zshrs-daemon ,
addressed via
.IR ~/.cache/zshrs/daemon.sock .
The daemon owns ALL mutation:
.IP "" 4
.B fsnotify
\(em watches
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
.BR $fpath ,
plugin trees, and invalidates the affected cache shards.
.IP "" 4
.B compile
\(em rkyv image writes for script_cache, plugin_cache, autoload_cache,
catalog.db rebuilds, log rotation, integrity scans.
.IP "" 4
.B IPC routing
\(em cross-shell pub/sub
.RB ( zsubscribe ,
.BR znotify ,
.BR zsend ,
.BR ztag ),
shell registry
.RB ( zls ,
.BR zid ,
.BR zping ),
and the session-persistent job supervisor
.RB ( zjob ,
planned).
.PP
100+ thin
.B zshrs
clients (e.g. one per tmux pane) mmap the daemon's outputs directly
for hot lookups (zero IPC), and use the socket only for control-plane
events (rebuild, fpath_changed, stats_flush, cross-shell dispatch).
Clients spawn zero background threads, polling timers, or SQLite
handles. See
.BR zd (1)
for daemon administration.
.PP
.B POSIX mode never spawns the daemon.
.SH "AHEAD-OF-TIME COMPILATION"
.B \-\-aot
.RI ( script.zsh )
emits a single self-contained executable that bundles
.BR fusevm
with the script's compiled bytecode. The result has zero runtime
dependency on the
.B zshrs
binary and can be distributed as a standalone artifact. AOT and the
daily-driver cache are two separate tracks: AOT for deploy binaries;
the rkyv cache for the interactive shell.
.SH "ASPECT-ORIENTED INTERCEPTS"
.B zshrs
supports
.BR before ,
.BR after ,
and
.B around
advice on every command, builtin, and user function. Registered via
the
.B intercepts
crate (see
.IR src/intercepts/ ),
the advice runs in-process inside fusevm \(em no
.BR fork (2)
\(em which makes it cheap enough for production tracing, profiling,
auditing, or per-command behaviour overlays. This is the foundation
for
.BR zpwrTrace ,
.BR zpwrFlame ,
.B zpwrTop
and the rest of the
.B zpwr
introspection stack.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
Standard zsh-recognised variables and the zshrs additions
.RB ( ZSHRS_CACHE ,
.BR ZSHRS_LOG ,
.BR ZSHRS_LONG_CMD_PRE_THRESHOLD )
are documented in
.BR zshrs (1).
.SH "FILES"
.TP
.I ~/.cache/zshrs/
Cache root. Sharded as
.IB {hash8} - {slug} .rkyv
per source root, plus
.BR catalog.db ,
.BR daemon.sock ,
.BR zshrs.log .
.TP
.I ~/.config/zshrs/zshrs-daemon.toml
Optional daemon config; see
.BR zd (1).
.TP
.I ~/.zshenv,\ ~/.zprofile,\ ~/.zshrc,\ ~/.zlogin,\ ~/.zlogout
Standard zsh startup files.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Identical to
.BR zsh (1).
The shell exits with the status of the last command, or with
.B $?
at the time of an
.B exit
call. 127 indicates command-not-found; 126 indicates a permission
error.
.SH "COMPATIBILITY GUARANTEES"
.B zshrs
treats the zsh upstream
.B C
source under
.IR src/zsh/Src/
as the spec floor. Bytecode and SQLite formats are versioned and
migration-safe; old caches either upgrade cleanly or rebuild
silently. A
.IR .zshrc " " "that worked yesterday"
must work today. Compat-floor regressions against
.BR zpwr ,
.BR zsh-more-completions ,
.BR oh-my-zsh ,
.BR zinit ,
.BR p10k ,
and the standard plugin set are catastrophic; the parity test corpus
.RB ( tests/zsh_compat_parity_gaps.rs ,
20,000+ cases) is gating.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Errors take the form
.BI "zshrs: " command ": " reason
on stderr. Informational chatter (init, indexing, autoload pre-warm,
plugin compile) goes to
.IR ~/.cache/zshrs/zshrs.log
via
.BR tracing ;
it is never printed to the terminal. No startup banner. No deprecation
nags. No spell-correction prompts. No "did you mean" hints.
.SH "DEBUGGING & INTROSPECTION"
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print fusevm opcodes before VM execution.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-tokens, \-\-dump\-ast, \-\-dump\-wordcode, \-\-dump\-zwc
Inspect each pipeline stage; see
.BR zshrs (1).
.TP
.B ZSHRS_LOG
.BR tracing -style filter on the structured log. Examples:
.B ZSHRS_LOG=debug
or
.BR "ZSHRS_LOG=zshrs::ported::lex=trace" .
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Full diagnostic report; useful when filing issues.
.SH "RECORDER"
.B zshrs-recorder
.RI ( bins/zshrs-recorder.rs )
captures shell sessions as a structured event stream. Feature-gated;
see
.BR docs/RECORDER.md " and " docs/RECORDER_PROTOCOL.md
for the on-disk schema.
.SH "WORKERS & NO-FORK ARCHITECTURE"
zshrs ships a persistent worker pool: long-lived processes that
service builtins, common externals
.RB ( ls ", " cat ", " grep ", "
etc., on the user's allowlist), and pipeline stages without invoking
.BR fork (2)
on every command.
.B "No fork, no problems."
This is invisible to scripts: pipelines compose, redirections work,
job control behaves identically. Workers can be disabled via
.BR setopt " " no_workers
or by the parity flags.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR zshrs (1),
.BR zd (1),
.BR zsh (1),
.BR zshall (1),
.BR zshbuiltins (1),
.BR zshoptions (1),
.BR zshparam (1),
.BR zshzle (1),
.BR zshcompsys (1),
.BR zshcompctl (1),
.BR zshmisc (1),
.BR zshmodules (1),
.BR zshroadmap (1),
.BR zshcalsys (1),
.BR zshtcpsys (1),
.BR zshcontrib (1),
.BR zshexpn (1)
.PP
